WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:01:24.990
Welcome to Biographics.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:33.590
Today’s highly recognizably protagonist
was hailed as genius of entertainment, a wizard

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:36.850
of animation, a pioneer of technology and
theme parks.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:41.500
The influence of Walt Disney on today’s
popular culture and collective imagination

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.150
cannot be understated.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:49.409
You can love him, hate him, or love to hate
him, but you cannot ignore his work.

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:53.960
Walt Disney once famously said that that laughter
is America’s greatest export.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.070
It’s not.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:02:00.119
It’s travel and transportation, with $236
Billion in sales in 2017.

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:06.799
But sale of intellectual property, including
TV and movies, amount to $49 Billion, which

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:09.970
is twice the GDP of Cyprus.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:14.760
So, if by laughter we mean entertainment,
then yes – American entertainment is one

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.780
of the country’s most important exports,
and these days, a good chunk of that intellectual

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:24.010
property is owned by Walt Disney Studios:
Pixar, Buenavista, and Miramax, plus the Marvel

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:29.310
and the Star Wars franchises, are some of
the largest and most visible entertainment

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.760
companies owned by the House of Mouse.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.860
Disney’s legacy can capture the imagination
of every girl or boy from early childhood

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.670
all the way through adulthood.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:43.720
And to think -- it all started with the doodle
of a mouse, sketched during a depressing train

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:44.730
ride home.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.980
But we’re getting ahead of ourselves...

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:56.110
Toilet Paper and Start Ups
Walter Elias Disney, later known as Uncle

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:59.950
Walt, was born on December 5, 1901, in Chicago,
Illinois.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:05.160
His parents, Flora and Elias, were farmers
and occasional entrepreneurs of Canadian origin.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.670
Walt was the youngest of five siblings, the
others being Ruth, Roy, Raymond and Herbert.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:14.470
His much older brothers would prove a source
of stability and inspiration later in life.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.870
At the age of seven, Walt and the Disneys
moved to Kansas City, where he spent most

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:19.000
of his childhood.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.230
This was an unhappy period of his life.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:25.390
Elias Sr had a hard time making a living,
switching from farming to distributing newspapers.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:30.620
In each of these activities, Walt and his
siblings were drafted to work hard and contribute

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:31.820
to the family’s welfare.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:36.600
Elias was a tough taskmaster and did not approve
of Walt trying to carve out time to do homework

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.030
or even attend school.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:42.910
You can probably imagine how Elias took to
his son’s budding talent for drawing – he

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:47.150
considered it a complete waste of time and
would not spend a cent on something as futile

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:48.470
as art supplies.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:52.230
Walt was reduced to drawing his first sketches
on toilet paper.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:56.290
Walt’s harsh relationship with his father
was mitigated by Walt’s close relationship

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:01.410
with his big brothers, especially Roy and
Herbert, who were an early source of encouragement.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:05.360
Unfortunately, Roy, Raymond and Herbert moved
out of the family home eventually, leaving

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.240
behind the much younger Walt.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:10.540
In 1917, the US joined the Entente in WWI.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.570
Alongside many other patriotic American boys,
Walt longed to do his part by donning a uniform

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:16.570
to serve in France.

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:20.120
He may have also had the added motivation
to leave his home behind.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:24.120
Considering the fact that he faked his age
to join the army at 16, Walt was likely trying

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.140
to escape from his home life.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.840
Unfortunately, he was rejected for being too
short.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:32.690
Walt had a Plan B: he successfully applied
to join the American Red Cross.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:36.372
While training as an ambulance driver, Walt
developed his artistic skills, and he became

00:04:36.372 --> 00:04:41.010
quite popular for decorating the ambulances
with cartoons and caricatures of fellow paramedics.

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:46.360
One of his fellow trainees was one Ray Kroc
– the legendary CEO of McDonald’s.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:51.530
Walt’s appetite for action remained unfulfilled;
the war was over before he was even deployed

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:52.530
overseas.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:56.590
But when he returned home, he had a good reason
to rejoice -- he found out he had won a scholarship

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:59.280
to attend the Kansas City Art Institute.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:04.370
After years of having to draw on toilet paper
and ambulances, the poor guy deserved a break.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.600
And proper art supplies!

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:11.590
While attending the Art Institute, Walt developed
a fascination for the growing genre of animation.

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:14.890
Animated shorts were commonly used at the
time in movie theatres as a crowd pleaser

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:16.210
before the main feature.

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:20.900
But they were crudely made, the soundtrack
was irrelevant and some of them were just

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:21.900
creepy as hell.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:26.410
Walt saw an opportunity to fill a potential
gap in the market with products of good quality.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:30.340
With a friend from the Art Institute, Ub Iwerks,
Walt set up his first animation company.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:34.590
It was a bold move, considering that the two
were nineteen and had little to no capital

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:35.590
to start with.

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:39.740
Walt and Ub developed a series of short animated
movies for the Newman chain of cinemas entitled

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.490
"Newman's Laugh-O-Grams".

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:46.730
The company was short lived, running from
1920 to 1923, but its cartoons were a hit

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:47.860
with audiences.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:52.490
Walt experimented with some of the themes
that would become a staple of his later production:

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:57.050
anthropomorphised animals, innovative takes
on classic fairy tales, a keen ear for popular

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:00.550
music – jazz at that time - and slapstick
humour.

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:04.770
Though Walt and Ub were talented animators,
as businessmen, they were, well … they were

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:05.920
pretty rubbish.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.150
The production costs of their shorts were
too high, and they signed unfair distribution

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:11.150
deals.

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:14.960
They thought they had hit jackpot when a distributor
company called Pictorial Clubs of Tennessee

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:19.940
promised $11,100 for six cartoons - $142,000
in today’s money.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:24.230
But Pictorial never paid, and the Laugh-o-gram
company soon went bankrupt.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:28.520
Interest asiding here: if today, some Laugh-o-grams
are still in tact and available, it’s thanks

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:33.180
to those Pictorial Clubs scoundrels – they
kept the six cartoons in their vault and reissued

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:35.340
them when Disney had become a household name.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:39.700
The fellow animator, and other employees of
Walt’s start-up, were about to call it quits.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:44.750
But Walt had a brilliant idea, with great
potential: how about mixing live action with

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:45.750
animation?

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:48.860
Walt’s pitch was to film a series of shorts
starring child actress Virginia Davis with

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:53.980
an innovative technique: mixing a live action
protagonist with animated characters and settings.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:58.820
Walt and Ub moved to California to start work
on this new series, entitled the "Alice Comedies".

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:02.660
Their early work attracted the attention of
a New York based distributor, Margaret J.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:03.660
Winkler.

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:07.370
Disney’s work was again plagued by high
production costs, which made the first Alice

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:08.650
Comedies unprofitable.

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:12.669
On Winkler’s insistence, Walt and Ub were
forced to increase their rate of production,

00:07:12.669 --> 00:07:14.650
while lowering the animators’ salaries.

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:18.190
By this time big brother Roy Disney had joined
the company and he seemed to have a much better

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:19.750
business sense than Walt.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:23.220
He would remain his business partner and trusted
advisor for the rest of his life.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:27.419
It was perhaps on Roy’s advice that Walt
and Ub decided to hire an entirely female

00:07:27.419 --> 00:07:29.000
crew of colourists.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:33.400
Traditional animation requires for each frame
of a cartoon to be coloured by hand.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:36.230
That’s 24 frames per second of footage.

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:41.090
So, it’s a meticulous, repetitive, time
consuming job requiring large numbers of skilled

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:42.090
professionals.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:46.010
At that time, women’s wages were much lower
than men, which meant big savings for the

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:47.010
Disney brothers.

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:49.700
One of the colourists was a young woman from
Idaho called Lillian.

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:53.550
At the end of a long day’s work, Walt would
routinely drive home a group of his employees.

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:57.400
Whatever the route, he would always make sure
that the last person to be dropped off was

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:59.740
Lillian, so that he could spend more time
alone with her.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:02.260
Lillian probably got the hint, and the two
started dating.

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:06.700
This led to a short engagement, and, eventually,
their wedding, in July of 1925.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:11.169
By 1927, American audiences had grown weary
of the Alice Comedies shorts, and Walt wanted

00:08:11.169 --> 00:08:12.870
to diversify his production.

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:16.270
He started working on a new character, Oswald
the Lucky Rabbit.

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:20.699
This series was successful, and Walt considered
rescinding his contract with Margaret Winkler

00:08:20.699 --> 00:08:22.690
to chance distribution on his own.

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:28.150
But in 1928, Walt discovered that Margaret
and her husband, Charles Mintz, later of Universal

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:31.630
Pictures, were masters of the fine print at
the bottom of contracts.

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:35.300
During a business meeting in New York, he
learned that, as part of his deal with Winkler,

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:38.710
every intellectual property developed within
the terms of the contract would be legally

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:40.990
owned by Winkler and Mintz.

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:44.960
Unbeknownst to him, the New York power couple
had another nasty trick in reserve.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:49.540
Their pushy demands and deadlines for the
Alice films alienated Disney’s animators.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:52.950
Most of them had vented their frustration
against Walt – rather than Winkler.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:57.040
So, when they eventually quit the company,
they were hired en masse by Universal!

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:00.020
Only Iwerks would remain by Walt’s side.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:04.250
While riding the train back to California
from New York, a depressed Walt Disney put

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:07.440
pencil to paper to soothe his sadness and
frustration.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:11.610
Little did he know, that doodle would change
his life, and have a huge impact on the lives

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:12.900
of millions of children.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:17.470
The character he sketched that day was the
immortal Mortimer Mouse.

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:24.890
House of Mouse
‘Mortimer who?’ you may ask.

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:30.010
Well, that character was Mickey Mouse, but
Walt’s initial choice of name was Mortimer.

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:34.220
Luckily Lillian talked him to his senses and
advised he change the name to Mickey.

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:38.150
If you are into this sort of thing, the name
“Mortimer” is of old French origin and

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:42.560
means ‘Dead Sea’ – not the cheeriest
of names for a talking mouse!

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:45.380
Walt’s company was now called Walt Disney
Cartoons.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:50.330
A skeleton crew which included only him and
Ub on drawing and animation duties, with Roy

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:54.250
supervising the business, and his wife and
Edna colouring and inking with Lillian.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:56.610
The gang soon delivered three Mickey Mouse
cartoons.

00:09:56.610 --> 00:09:57.910
The first two did not sell.

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:03.120
For the third one, Walt had the intuition
to add a recent cinematic innovation: a synchronized

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.940
soundtrack, which included a catchy whistling
tune.

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:07.100
This third cartoon was ‘Steamboat Willie.’

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:13.310
It opened on November 18, 1928 and it was
an immediate success for Walt Disney Cartoons.

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:17.260
Finally the big break had arrived and the
company would produce a string of hit animated

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:18.260
shorts.

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:21.830
In 1929, they created the cartoon series 'Silly
Symphonies'.

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:26.320
A 1932 episode, Flowers and Trees, was the
first cartoon to be produced in colour and

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:27.320
to win an Oscar.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:32.180
The follow up, the Three Little Pigs of 1933,
was so popular that it got top billing above

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:34.150
the feature films it accompanied.

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:37.970
While working on the Silly Symphonies, Walt
and Lillian were trying to conceive a child.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:42.339
Unfortunately Lillian suffered several miscarriages
which caused Walt to experience a nervous

00:10:42.339 --> 00:10:43.339
breakdown.

00:10:43.339 --> 00:10:46.320
Eventually, a baby girl named Diane arrived
in 1933.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:49.980
The Disneys decided to adopt another girl,
Sharon, in 1936.

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:54.089
The successes of his cartoons and a growing
family emboldened Walt, who now started to

00:10:54.089 --> 00:10:59.180
think big: why should cartoons be developed
only in short format?

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:02.310
Why not produce a full-length feature animated
film?

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:10.490
Your wish will soon come true
In 1934, Walt started working on that idea:

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:13.670
a cartoon that ran the length of a feature
film.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:18.720
As he had done previously, Walt sought inspiration
from a classic Fairy Tale: Snow White by the

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:19.720
Brothers Grimm.

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:21.590
Nobody in Hollywood believed in Walt.

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:25.940
Among the studio lots, film executives sneered
at the project, calling it "Disney's Folly".

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.480
The production was not easy, going over time
and over budget.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:34.540
But when Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs opened
on the December 21, 1937 it won immediately

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:38.020
the hearts and minds of both audiences and
critics.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:42.140
Including subsequent releases, and adjusting
for inflation, Snow White is the tenth highest

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:44.600
grossing movie of all times.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:49.240
At the 1938 Academy Awards, the film was nominated
for best soundtrack and received a Special

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:53.620
Award for ‘significant screen innovation’
– in the shape of one normal-sized Oscar

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:56.310
and seven little statuettes.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:12:00.670
Walt Disney Studios continued working on shorts,
but the main production focus was firmly on

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:02.230
feature-length cartoons.

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:06.350
Walt’s next two projects were incredibly
ambitious: he released Pinocchio and Fantasia,

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:07.550
both in 1940.

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:12.630
Pinocchio was a relative success – not as
astounding as Snow White - but Fantasia tanked.

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:14.720
If you haven’t seen it yet, press pause,
go and find a DVD, a VHS, a Betamax, whatever,

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:15.720
and watch it now.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:16.720
Welcome back.

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:17.720
Seen it?

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:18.720
Did you like it?

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:19.720
I told you it was awesome!

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:21.270
But 1940 audiences did not agree with our
modern sensibilities, and for the next production,

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:24.990
Dumbo, Walt had to tighten the belt and reduce
costs to a minimum.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:28.750
Dumbo’s artwork and animation are visibly
cruder than the three previous movies, and

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:32.860
the runtime is also quite short – but the
film was highly profitable.

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:36.970
This relative reduction in quality may have
also been a consequence of a studio animators’

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:39.980
strike, an event which deeply angered and
worried Disney.

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:43.510
Boss Disney
A 2006 biography of Walt Disney by Neal Gabler

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:48.260
sheds some light on Walt’s relationship
with his employees, trade unions, and communism.

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:52.100
To put it simply, Walt Disney was a tough
boss to work for: it is no surprise that his

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:56.370
employees wanted to organise themselves in
a union, but he considered industrial action

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:59.210
as a dangerous expression of communist tendencies.

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:00.880
Think of this example.

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:05.440
When Walt turned 35, his brother Roy encouraged
employees to throw the boss a surprise birthday

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:06.440
party.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:09.260
Two of the animators thought it would be a
fun idea to create a short cartoon of Mickey

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:10.840
and Minnie Mouse … going at it.

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:11.840
(Wink wink, nudge nudge.)

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:15.130
When Walt saw the animation at the party,
he laughed hard and asked who had made the

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:16.130
film.

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:18.690
The two animators stepped up, expecting a
pat on the back or a handshake.

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:20.480
He fired them on the spot.

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:23.790
In general, Walt could be controlling and
asked a lot from his employees, resorting

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:26.550
to humiliating dress-downs if they did not
deliver.

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:27.709
Even Roy was not spared.

00:13:27.709 --> 00:13:31.600
The business brain behind Walt Disney studios
was frequently scolded when he offered an

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:33.330
opinion on artistic choices.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:38.130
Uncle Walt’s employees, animators, and cartoonists
were the ones subject to longest hours and

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:39.350
gruelling work conditions.

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:43.170
Taking a break was a luxury, as Walt would
often appear suddenly in the drawing rooms

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:44.320
to check on their work.

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:48.399
Luckily for the employees, Walt was a heavy
smoker: with time he had developed a persistent

00:13:48.399 --> 00:13:51.550
cough that announced his arrival by a few
seconds!

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:56.770
When his cartoonists tried to form a union,
Walt reacted by hiring armed guards, firing

00:13:56.770 --> 00:13:59.080
organisers and cutting wages.

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:02.220
Well, the 1941 strike … but he really asked
for it!

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:07.200
On the July 2, 1941 Disney published an advert
in Variety, accusing the strike leaders of

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:08.730
‘Communistic agitation’.

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:12.580
This concern about Communist infiltrations
in Hollywood led him to join the Motion Picture

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:16.430
Alliance for the Preservation of American
Ideals, or MPA.

00:14:16.430 --> 00:14:21.850
Founded in February 1944, the MPA was an organisation
composed of high-profile showbiz personalities

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:26.450
with the purpose of defending Hollywood – and
America as a whole – against Communism and

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:27.450
Fascism.

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:31.560
The MPA was active until 1975 and notable
members included John Wayne, Ronald Reagan

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.930
and Ginger Rogers to name a few.

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:37.899
The MPA volunteered to testify in front of
the House Committee on Un-American Activities.

00:14:37.899 --> 00:14:42.920
The Committee was created in 1938 to investigate
alleged disloyalty and subversive activities

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:48.070
on the part of private citizens, public employees,
and organizations suspected of having Communist

00:14:48.070 --> 00:14:49.070
ties.

00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:52.240
Walt also participated in the hearings of
the committee, accusing several leaders of

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:55.080
the animators’ strike of being Communist
agitators.

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:59.529
His testimony earned him the gratitude and
friendship of legendary FBI Director J. Edgar

00:14:59.529 --> 00:15:03.220
Hoover – one whose life we’ll tell sooner
or later.

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:06.080
Hoover and Disney developed a strange artistic
partnership.

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:10.050
In the following years, J. Edgar allowed Walt
Disney Studios to film some of their live

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.770
action movies at the FBI headquarters in Washington.

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:16.240
Walt, on the other hand, agreed to submit
some of his scripts to Hoover for an early

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:20.850
revision, to ensure that the FBI was depicted
correctly, but apparently, Hoover did not

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:23.080
make any changes to the classic animated films.

00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:24.080
This is an odd refrain he would have slashed
half of Peter Pan’s script for depicting

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:25.080
an anarchist commune and abuse of substances
that literally get you flying.

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:27.380
In addition to these exchanges of favours,
Hoover had Disney appointed as ‘Special

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:29.790
Agent in Charge Contact’ in 1954.

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:33.890
In other words: a trusted informer and collaborator
of the FBI.

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:38.190
Fun fact – decades after Walt Disney’s
death, his company would pay homage to the

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:43.480
FBI chief with a minor character: J. Gander
Hooter from TV series Darkwing Duck.

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:48.620
In the 1950s Walt Disney eventually distanced
himself from the MPA, as he lost interest

00:15:48.620 --> 00:15:51.600
in their ideals and their paranoid approach
to anti-Communism.

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:56.450
Walt’s association with the MPA is the source
of the rumour that he was anti-Semitic, but

00:15:56.450 --> 00:16:00.990
biographer Neal Gabler, otherwise a harsh
critic of Disney’s, dismisses this claim

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:02.600
as unsubstantiated.

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:06.580
Walt regularly hired, or had business with,
Jewish employees and colleagues.

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:11.100
In 1955 he was even appointed ‘man of the
year’ by the B’nai B’rith International,

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:13.459
a Jewish cultural organisation.

00:16:13.459 --> 00:16:17.460
This is not the only persistent, unfounded
rumour on Walt, more on this later.

00:16:17.460 --> 00:16:27.671
A Triumph of the Imagination
In the 1950s and 1960s Walt Disney studios

00:16:27.671 --> 00:16:32.180
produced a series of animated masterpieces
that won well-deserved popular success.

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:35.459
I’ll list them all, because I know if I
miss one, I’ll get backlash in the comments:

00:16:35.459 --> 00:16:41.010
Cinderella (1950), Alice in Wonderland (1951),
Peter Pan (1953), Lady and the Tramp (1955),

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:47.060
Sleeping Beauty (1959), 101 Dalmatians (1961)
and Mary Poppins (1964).

00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:48.220
Everybody has a favourite of course, but what
about your least favourite?

00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:49.220
Write your vote in the comments and tell us
why you chose it!

00:16:49.220 --> 00:16:50.220
My personal favourite?

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:51.220
Why! ‘tis Mary Poppins of course!

00:16:51.220 --> 00:16:52.220
Not only Dick Van Dyke’s spot on cockney
accent reminds me of my relatives in London

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:53.220
… but it’s Disney’s first superhero
movie if you think about it – long before

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:54.220
the Marvel acquisition came along.

00:16:54.220 --> 00:16:55.220
Mary has the powers of
Flight

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:56.220
Telekinesis
Interdimensional travel

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:57.220
Mind manipulation through the medium of song
Infinite storage capabilities

00:16:57.220 --> 00:16:58.220
So, yeah, studio executives, when you are
planning the next Avengers film, how about

00:16:58.220 --> 00:16:59.220
you draft in Poppins, too?

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:00.220
Mary Poppins successfully merged live action
and animation in colour.

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:01.220
Before that, Disney studios had ventured into
purely live action films, the first one being

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:02.220
Treasure Island in 1950.

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:04.939
Disney was also one of the first Hollywood
producers to invest in TV.

00:17:04.939 --> 00:17:07.909
The first of his series on the small screen
was The Magical World of Disney – which

00:17:07.909 --> 00:17:12.909
actually can be considered an early example
of Content Marketing to promote a good or

00:17:12.909 --> 00:17:13.909
service.

00:17:13.909 --> 00:17:14.909
What was Disney promoting?

00:17:14.909 --> 00:17:19.439
His next big dream, the one that would take
most of his energy and attention for the last

00:17:19.439 --> 00:17:26.639
decade of his life, was a massive, innovative
theme park in Southern California: Disneyland.

00:17:26.639 --> 00:17:30.850
The park was developed in the town of Anaheim,
after demographics experts convinced Walt

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:34.190
that it would become a major population centre
within 10 years.

00:17:34.190 --> 00:17:39.940
Tme would prove they were right, when local
population, and park visitors, soared.

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:45.220
Disneyland opened on the July 17, 1955 and
its first day was a disaster.

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:50.389
30,000 people turned up instead of the projected
15,000, meaning restaurants were soon out

00:17:50.389 --> 00:17:51.450
of food and drink.

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:56.470
A plumber’s strike forced Walt to choose:
either have flushing toilets or working drinking

00:17:56.470 --> 00:17:57.470
fountains.

00:17:57.470 --> 00:17:58.910
He chose the toilets.

00:17:58.910 --> 00:18:03.639
One of the attractions was so overgrown with
weeds that Disney ordered to place around

00:18:03.639 --> 00:18:04.899
placards with Latin names.

00:18:04.899 --> 00:18:08.009
To disguise the shrubbery as an arboretum,
in other words!

00:18:08.009 --> 00:18:10.879
Of course, everything improved, and Disneyland
became one of the most, if not the most visited

00:18:10.879 --> 00:18:12.799
and successful theme parks Worldwide.

00:18:12.799 --> 00:18:16.950
When looking at visitors’ stats, Disney
realised that only a small fraction came from

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:17.950
the West Coast.

00:18:17.950 --> 00:18:21.700
That’s when he got the idea of building
a sister park in Florida, Disney World, developed

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:25.850
around the prototype of a futuristic perfect
city, called EPCOT.

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:28.559
But Walt Disney would not live to see his
new dream come true.

00:18:28.559 --> 00:18:32.269
For most of his adult life he had been smoking
three packs of unfiltered cigarettes, every

00:18:32.269 --> 00:18:33.269
day.

00:18:33.269 --> 00:18:36.289
His daughter Diane tried, and failed, to convince
him to cut back.

00:18:36.289 --> 00:18:40.710
They reached a compromise: he would at least
smoke three packs of filtered cigarettes – but

00:18:40.710 --> 00:18:43.860
he just removed the filters behind Diane’s
back!

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:47.169
Inevitably in 1966, Walt Disney was diagnosed
with lung cancer.

00:18:47.169 --> 00:18:51.629
He underwent surgery, but due to post-operative
complications, Walt had a heart attack and

00:18:51.629 --> 00:18:56.519
died on December 15, 1966 at age 65.

00:18:56.519 --> 00:19:01.110
Contrary to popular and persistent rumours,
his body was NOT cryogenically preserved and/or

00:19:01.110 --> 00:19:02.919
hidden in a vault under Disneyland.

00:19:02.919 --> 00:19:09.330
Walt was simply cremated, and his ashes were
interred at Forest Lawn Cemetery in Los Angeles,

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:10.519
California.

00:19:10.519 --> 00:19:16.490
Legacy
I hope you forgive me for stating the obvious,

00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:21.809
but Walt Disney was, and still is, a controversial
figure, as is normal for somebody whose influence

00:19:21.809 --> 00:19:25.059
on the surrounding world and society is larger
than life.

00:19:25.059 --> 00:19:30.690
A personality like Disney will never be free
from exaggerations, slander and rumours – or,

00:19:30.690 --> 00:19:34.830
the opposite risk, is to portray him exclusively
in a saintly light.

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:38.090
For sure, Walt Disney was certainly not the
fairest of business leaders, not the most

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:42.629
balanced of workplace bosses, not the most
level-headed when confronted with different

00:19:42.629 --> 00:19:43.730
political ideas.

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:48.710
But it is also true that he did deliver a
lasting impact on society, culture and children

00:19:48.710 --> 00:19:50.169
through his innovative work.

00:19:50.169 --> 00:19:53.370
As Walt once said:
“I'd rather entertain and hope that people

00:19:53.370 --> 00:20:20.360
learn, than teach and hope that people are
entertained”

